### Summary of Key Insights
The retrieved evidence shows that the physiological transfer of blood from the placenta to the newborn is completed very quickly, plateauing and ceasing at approximately 140 seconds (about 2.3 minutes) after birth. Consequently, international health guidelines from the WHO and ACOG recommend delaying cord clamping for only 30 seconds to 3 minutes to ensure the baby receives sufficient blood volume and iron stores. There is no scientific evidence supporting the need to wait one hour to cut the cord. Furthermore, waiting an hour is not clinically recommended and can be dangerous in situations requiring immediate medical intervention for the mother or the baby. Because the evidence is clear and definitive, no further investigation is required.

### Conclusion
Based on the fact-check, the claim that the umbilical cord must not be cut for an hour to ensure the baby has enough blood is medically and physiologically incorrect. Scientific evidence demonstrates that placental transfusion is fully completed within a few minutes of birth, and delaying clamping for 1 to 3 minutes is sufficient to provide all the necessary blood volume. Waiting an hour is not recommended by any major medical organization and could be harmful in emergencies. Therefore, the final decision is that the claim is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
Medical guidelines and scientific evidence demonstrate that waiting an hour to cut a newborn's umbilical cord is medically unnecessary and unsupported. According to a study in [PMC](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4687772/), the physiological transfer of placental blood to the newborn plateaus and ceases approximately 140 seconds (about 2.3 minutes) after birth, making a brief delay of three minutes sufficient to maximize blood volume and prevent anemia. Consequently, the [World Health Organization (WHO)](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK310522/) recommends delaying cord clamping for 1 to 3 minutes, and the [American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (ACOG)](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/experts-and-stories/ask-acog/what-is-delayed-cord-clamping) suggests a delay of 30 to 60 seconds, which a study in the [Italian Journal of Pediatrics](https://ijponline.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13052-020-00835-2) notes is associated with improved hemoglobin levels and iron storage. Furthermore, delaying clamping for an hour is clinically contraindicated in emergencies, such as maternal hemorrhaging or when a newborn requires immediate resuscitation, where immediate clamping is necessary to ensure safety ([Healthline](https://www.healthline.com/health/pregnancy/delayed-cord-clamping)).